WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.250
A student team reports that their coding

2
00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:07.090
agent solves ninety percent of tasks.

3
00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:11.330
When you rerun it, it solves sixty.

4
00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:14.690
They ran it once, on tasks they

5
00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:15.890
wrote while building it.

6
00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:17.720
What should they have done?

7
00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:21.540
Today: how to measure whether an agent

8
00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:25.640
works, what pass at k really means, and

9
00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:27.760
how to teach students to report results

10
00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:29.360
that others can trust.

11
00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:32.940
An evaluation runs an agent on a

12
00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:34.920
fixed set of tasks with an

13
00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:36.320
automatic check of success.

14
00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:39.160
The tasks must be separate from

15
00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:40.680
those used to build the agent.

16
00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:43.610
And because agents are random, it

17
00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:45.150
must be run more than once.

18
00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:49.360
pass at k is the probability that at

19
00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:51.460
least one of k attempts succeeds.

20
00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:54.460
If one attempt succeeds with

21
00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:58.340
probability p, pass at k is one

22
00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:01.860
minus one minus p, to the power k.

23
00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:05.220
It rewards retries, so report

24
00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:06.640
pass at one as well.

25
00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:10.380
On fifty tasks, a sixty percent

26
00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:12.540
success rate has a standard error

27
00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:14.240
of about seven points.

28
00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:18.150
So ninety and sixty may partly be

29
00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:20.210
noise and partly overfitting.

30
00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:22.700
Report the mean over several

31
00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:23.920
runs with an interval.

32
00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.060
Public benchmarks may already

33
00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:28.520
appear in a model's training

34
00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:30.200
data, which inflates scores.

35
00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:33.290
Agents can also game a weak checker,

36
00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:35.490
for example by editing the tests.

37
00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:38.860
Hold out fresh tasks and inspect

38
00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:41.240
failures and successes by hand.

39
00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:45.360
In student agent projects, grade the

40
00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:48.260
evaluation as seriously as the agent.

41
00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:52.300
Require a held-out task set, several

42
00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:55.100
runs, pass at one with an interval,

43
00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:57.720
and a short error analysis.

44
00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:01.690
A modest honest result should earn

45
00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:03.410
more than an inflated one.

46
00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:05.190
Quick check!

47
00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:09.140
An agent succeeds on a single attempt

48
00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:12.360
with probability zero point two.

49
00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:15.110
What is pass at two?

50
00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:18.960
Zero point three six: one minus

51
00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:20.620
zero point eight squared.

52
00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:23.680
Two tries nearly double the headline

53
00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:26.600
number, which is why pass at one matters.

54
00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:29.870
In the app, open the evals lab.

55
00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:32.840
Run an agent several times on the same

56
00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:35.720
tasks, watch the score move, and

57
00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:38.220
compare pass at one with pass at k.

58
00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:39.920
Your turn.

59
00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:43.250
Which result is most trustworthy

60
00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:45.550
for a student agent project?

61
00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:49.830
Pass at one averaged over five runs on

62
00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:51.710
held-out tasks, with an interval.

63
00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:55.020
Remember: separate test tasks,

64
00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:57.620
several runs, honest metrics,

65
00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:00.040
and read the transcripts.

66
00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:03.520
Try it in your next lecture: show two

67
00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:06.420
teams' results, one inflated and one

68
00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:08.580
honest, without saying which is which.

69
00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:11.350
Pairs list what each team

70
00:03:11.350 --> 00:03:13.190
did and did not report.

71
00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:16.320
Then vote on which result they trust.
